/-
# Problem Description

Erdős Problem 48. Are there infinitely many integers `n, m` such that `φ(n) = σ(m)`?
`erdos_48` proves that there are.

The question appears in [Er59c], [Er74b], [ErGr80] and [Er95]; it would follow immediately
from the twin prime conjecture. The affirmative answer is due to Ford, Luca and Pomerance
[FLP10], who in fact obtain at least `exp((log log x)^c)` such values up to `x`. Below, `φ` is
`Nat.totient` and `σ 1` the sum-of-divisors function, and the statement is that the set of
pairs `(n, m)` with `φ(n) = σ(m)` is infinite.

The formalisation is by plby (github.com/plby/lean-proofs),
`src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos48.lean` together with the modules of its import closure.
Those files are concatenated here in dependency order, with their project-internal imports
removed so that `Mathlib` is the only import, each module's contents kept in a `section`
carrying its own `open` lines, and the whole wrapped once in `namespace Erdos48` with the
upstream trust-base print line removed. No mathematical content is changed.
-/

